    "body": "Image processed by CodeCarvings Piczard ### FREE Community Edition ### on 2021-03-03 07:28:12Z | http://piczard.com | http://codecarvings.com¾M ^DÂ\nBy Gavin van Marle\n18 November 2026\nMSC’s East Mediterranean transhipment hub, in the Turkish port of Tekirdag, is set to almost triple the handling capacity at its Asyaport facility.\nThere are plans to expand the Asyaport terminal, which is 75% owned by MSC, and 25% by the local Soyuer Group, and increase capacity from the current 2.75m teu to 4m teu by the end of the year.\nThe terminal’s deepsea berths are being extended from 1km to 1.4km, while an adjacent feeder pier has already been extended, to 710 metres.\nThe fresh capacity is needed by both port and carrier – according to Xeneta’s eeSea liner database, Asyaport handled 2.2m teu last year, representing an annual utilisation rate of 78%, pushing the facility into territory where outbreaks of congestion can be difficult to clear.\nThroughput this year hit 1.86m teu in the first 10 months, which analysts at Alphaliner suggested “the port is on course to exceed last year’s record”.\nThey noted: “The Turkish port exclusively handles MSC vessels and operates its own towage and pilotage services. Transhipment accounts for 80% of its throughput, leaving 10% each for exports and imports from and into Turkey via the European side of the Marmara Sea.”\nAccording to eeSea, MSC has some 14 feeder and intra-Europe services calling at the terminal, using it as its gateway to the Black Sea, as well as its Asia-Europe Tiger service, operated in conjunction with Premier Alliance members, and transatlantic EMUSA service.\nMeanwhile, according to Alphaliner, a third and fourth expansion phase have already been developed by the terminal, with planning approvals in place, that would ultimately take Asyaport’s capacity up to 6m teu a year, and cement its place as the largest container facility in the country.\nImage: Alphaliner\n“A third expansion phase, for which the necessary permits are prepared, would sacrifice a 310-metre feeder berth for land reclamation, increasing the container yard area with another 30,000 sq metres,” Alphaliner writes.\n“The fourth phase would see the length of both the deepwater and feeder pier extended by 700 metres, bringing the total quay length to 2,810 metres.\n“Timing will depend on future cargo demand at Tekirdag,” the analyst added.",
